# Illustration

Use an illustration to explain something that words alone make hard: where a sensor goes in a room, how a phone reaches a hub, or the steps of a bedtime routine. Put the full explanation in text beside it. For the colours to use, see [Illustration colours](colour-illustrations.html).

## Why use illustrations

A drawing of a hall and a motion sensor explains placement faster than a paragraph, and helps people who find long text hard. One shared style also makes Civica recognisable and means contributors draw less.

<h2 id="bg-ill-01">When to use an illustration</h2>

Decide the message and the task first. Then decide whether a picture helps. Use an illustration to show:

- a place, such as where to mount a temperature sensor on a floor plan
- a relationship, such as a phone, Home Assistant, a hub and a sensor
- a sequence, such as the steps of a routine that turns the heating down at night

If the task needs a real state, a photograph or a reading, use an icon, an image or a chart instead.

## Illustrations, icons and photographs

An illustration tells a story. An icon labels one thing. A photograph shows something real.

<h3 id="bg-ill-07">Icons</h3>

Use Home Assistant’s own icons for devices, entities and actions, with their state variants. [Icons](images-icons.html) owns how to choose and label icons.

This example shows a navigation arrow. It keeps its outline because it is a symbol, not an illustration.

